---
import fs from "node:fs";
import path from "node:path";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PhotoSlot from "../../components/PhotoSlot.astro";
import VideoSlot from "../../components/VideoSlot.astro";

const exists = (p: string) => fs.existsSync(path.join(process.cwd(), "public", p));
const heroExists = exists("images/safe.webp");
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="The Jentosy Project is a 501(c)(3) nonprofit advancing kidney health equity for underserved communities, starting in the Bronx."
>
  <!-- HERO — image only, no text on the photo. A light, flat tint (not a
       fading gradient) keeps it feeling of a piece with the site without
       the photo washing out at the bottom. Crisp, deliberate cut into
       the article below — no blend. -->
  <section class="on-dark about-hero" data-parallax-section>
    {heroExists ? (
      <img class="about-hero-bg" src="/images/safe.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" data-parallax-bg />
    ) : (
      <div class="about-hero-placeholder">
        <p class="about-hero-placeholder-note">
          Hero photo placeholder — drop <code>safe.webp</code> into <code>public/images/</code>{" "}
          and it'll appear here automatically.
        </p>
      </div>
    )}
    <div class="about-hero-tint"></div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE ARTICLE — one continuous piece, not a stack of sections. ABOUT
       sits immediately under the hero, then the headline and the story
       begin right underneath it. -->
  <article class="on-light about-article">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="article-kicker reveal" aria-hidden="true">About</p>
      <h1 class="article-title reveal" style="--reveal-delay:0.06s">
        Geography decides who knows in time.
      </h1>

      <p class="reveal" style="--reveal-delay:0.1s">
        Kidney disease rarely announces itself. It can move through the body for years, sometimes
        decades, without a single symptom worth mentioning to a doctor. By the time most people
        feel that something is wrong, a third of their kidney function may already be gone.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal" style="--reveal-delay:0.14s">
        What determines whether someone catches it in time has less to do with biology than with
        geography. Where a person lives shapes what they know about their own risk long before it
        shapes what care they can get. A zip code can predict, with unsettling accuracy, whether
        someone grows up hearing the words <em>family history of kidney disease</em> at all.
      </p>

      <p class="pull reveal">
        This isn't an accident of nature. It's the compounded result of poverty, of schools that
        never taught the basics of kidney health, of neighborhoods without a nephrologist within
        reach, of generations who learned, reasonably, not to trust a medical system that hadn't
        earned it.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal">
        Kidney disease doesn't discriminate on its own. The systems around it do.
      </p>

      <!-- THE ORGANIZATION -->
      <p class="article-kicker chapter reveal">The Organization</p>
      <h2 class="article-heading reveal">Built to close that gap.</h2>

      <p class="reveal">
        The Jentosy Project exists to change what people know before it's too late to act on it.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        It is an education-focused nonprofit — not a research lab, not a treatment center, not a
        fundraising engine for someone else's cure. Its job is narrower, and, its founder would
        argue, more urgent: making sure people in underserved communities understand kidney
        disease early enough for that understanding to matter.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Education sounds modest next to words like <em>cure</em> or <em>transplant</em>. It isn't.
        Chronic kidney disease is usually treatable, sometimes even reversible, if it's caught
        early. The failure, more often than not, isn't medical. It's informational. People don't
        get the test because no one told them they were at risk. They don't ask the question
        because no one gave them the words for it. That is the gap this organization was built to
        close — the specifics of how live on the
        <a href="/about/what-we-do/">What We Do</a> page.
      </p>

      <!-- WHY THE BRONX -->
      <p class="article-kicker chapter reveal">Why the Bronx</p>
      <h2 class="article-heading reveal">A starting point, not an endpoint.</h2>

      <p class="reveal">
        The work begins in the Bronx — not because the Bronx is exceptional, but because it is
        representative.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        The borough carries some of the starkest health disparities in the country: elevated rates
        of diabetes and hypertension, the two leading drivers of kidney failure; a population
        disproportionately affected by APOL1 gene variants; neighborhoods where the nearest
        nephrologist might as well be in another state. Almost every one of those facts has a twin
        in dozens of other communities — Detroit, rural Mississippi, South Los Angeles.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Starting in the Bronx is a decision about where to prove the model works, not where the
        problem ends. What's built here — the language, the trust, the relationships with clinics
        and community groups — is meant to travel.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="article-image reveal">
      <PhotoSlot src="/images/movement.webp" alt="A community of people, rendered in warm earth tones, standing together" ratio="3 / 2" />
      <figcaption>A community, not a case study.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="container narrow">
      <!-- THE NAME -->
      <p class="article-kicker chapter reveal">The Name</p>
      <h2 class="article-heading reveal">Two women. One chain. A debt worth a lifetime.</h2>

      <p class="reveal">
        The organization's name is not an acronym, and it isn't a place. It's two people.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        In 2009, Robert Sanchez — the man who would eventually found The Jentosy Project —
        received his first kidney transplant, a living donation from a close friend. FSGS, the
        rare disease that had already destroyed his own kidneys, found its way into the new one.
        By 2014 he was back on dialysis, three times a week, watching a machine do what his body
        no longer could.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        In 2019, out of options, he did something simple and a little desperate: he posted on
        Facebook and asked if anyone knew someone willing to donate a kidney. Within about a week,
        an organ matchmaker named Chaya Lipschutz had found someone. Her name was Elizabeth
        Antosy.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Then, the night before surgery, everything changed again. Robert's transplant surgeon
        called with the kind of news no one is prepared for — a diagnosis of kidney cancer, found
        too late to operate. The transplant was off.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Most people, reasonably, would have walked away. Elizabeth didn't. She joined a kidney
        exchange chain instead — a system where a willing but incompatible donor is matched,
        through a series of linked transplants, with a stranger elsewhere in the chain, so that
        stranger's own donor can help someone new in turn. Once Robert had cleared cancer, that
        chain matched him with another donor: Jen Keller. On October 20, 2021, the two of them
        were transplanted. Elizabeth's decision to stay in the chain rather than walk away
        ultimately gave <strong>eight people</strong> new kidneys.
      </p>

      <p class="pull reveal">The organization is named for both of them — Jen, and An-tosy.</p>

      <figure class="story-video-figure reveal">
        <VideoSlot
          src="/videos/where-the-name-comes-from.mp4"
          youtubeId="nsQ35ZgiWOk"
          ratio="16 / 9"
          title="Bronx man finally getting kidney transplant after years of waiting — CBS News New York"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Robert on Jen, Elizabeth, and the chain that connected them. Independently covered by{" "}
          <a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/newyork/news/kidney-transplant-kidney-chain-robert-sanchez-elizabeth-antosy/">CBS News New York</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="reveal">
        Robert tells this story often, in more detail than fits here, because he believes the
        specifics matter — not just that two women helped him, but who they were, what it cost
        them, and what it means to build an organization in the shape of their generosity. Read
        the extended version on the <a href="/about/team/">Meet The Team</a> page.
      </p>

      <!-- STORYTELLING AS MEDICINE -->
      <p class="article-kicker chapter reveal">Storytelling as Medicine</p>
      <h2 class="article-heading reveal">Data tells you where. Stories tell you why.</h2>

      <p class="reveal">
        Ask Robert why the organization leads with stories instead of statistics, and he'll tell
        you: the statistics never explained anything to him. Not really.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Data tells you where a problem exists. It rarely tells you why. A chart showing that Black
        Americans develop kidney failure at four times the rate of white Americans is true, and
        important, and almost useless to a person trying to understand what that means for their
        own body, their own family, their own next doctor's visit. What changed things for
        Robert — what turned a diagnosis into something he could act on — wasn't a statistic. It
        was hearing from someone who had already lived through it.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        That's the premise the organization is built on: storytelling isn't an add-on to the work
        of health education. It is the work.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Stories do things data cannot. They reduce fear by replacing the unknown with the
        specific — this is what it felt like, this is what happened next, this is how I got
        through it. They build trust in a medical system that has, for many communities, not yet
        earned it, because a shared story carries a kind of credibility a pamphlet never will.
        They turn a diagnosis into something survivable, because someone else already survived it.
        And they remind the person hearing them that they are, whatever the moment feels like,
        more than a diagnosis.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="article-image article-image-inset reveal">
      <PhotoSlot src="/images/healingheart.webp" alt="A heart formed from interlocking human silhouettes, in warm earth tones" ratio="1 / 1" />
      <figcaption>Lived experience, treated as evidence.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="reveal">
        Patients who hear from peers with similar diagnoses report lower anxiety, more confidence
        in their treatment decisions, and stronger relationships with their care teams. For
        communities carrying generations of reasonable mistrust toward healthcare institutions, a
        story can do what an outreach campaign can't — because it isn't asking to be believed. It
        just is.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        The Jentosy Project treats lived experience as a form of evidence — not instead of
        clinical data, but alongside it. Robert's story, and the stories of the people who will
        eventually join him in telling theirs, aren't testimonials bolted onto the mission. They
        are the mission, in practice: proof that this disease can be understood, survived, and
        outrun, told by someone who has done it.
      </p>

      <!-- MISSION + VISION, woven in -->
      <p class="reveal">
        All of this adds up to something the organization states plainly, without embellishment:
        The Jentosy Project advances health equity for underserved communities by educating people
        about rare kidney diseases — including FSGS and APOL1-mediated kidney disease — and
        building the partnerships needed to close the gap in kidney care. It isn't a complicated
        mission. It doesn't need to be.
      </p>

      <p class="pull reveal">
        The vision behind it is just as direct: a future where no one's zip code determines their
        access to kidney health education, early detection, or life-saving care.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal">
        Getting there starts, again, with education — because knowledge is the one part of this
        equation that doesn't require insurance, or a referral, or proximity to a research
        hospital. It just requires someone willing to explain it, and someone willing to listen.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="article-image article-image-inset reveal">
      <PhotoSlot src="/images/treeoflife.webp" alt="Cupped hands cradling a tree that grows from a pair of kidneys" ratio="5 / 4" />
      <figcaption>Rooted in care, growing toward equity.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="container narrow">
      <!-- WHY EDUCATION COMES FIRST -->
      <p class="article-kicker chapter reveal">Why Education Comes First</p>
      <h2 class="article-heading reveal">Prevention only works before the crisis.</h2>

      <p class="reveal">
        It would be easier, in some ways, to build an organization around transplantation. It's
        the part of Robert's story with the clearest emotional arc — the crisis, the donor, the
        surgery, the recovery. But by the time someone needs a transplant, the moment where
        education could have changed the outcome has usually already passed.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Most kidney disease doesn't announce a deadline. It gives warnings, if anyone is paying
        attention: protein in the urine, blood pressure that won't come down, family members who
        died young of something no one ever named precisely. Catching those warnings takes two
        things that sound simple and rarely are — knowing what to look for, and having somewhere
        to bring the question.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Health literacy is the first. A person who understands what FSGS is, or what it means to
        carry two copies of a high-risk APOL1 variant, is a person who can ask their doctor the
        right question at the right appointment — instead of learning the term for the first time
        in a hospital bed. Community education, delivered in plain language by people the
        community already trusts, is how that understanding actually spreads: faster and further
        than any pamphlet in a waiting room.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Trust is the second, and the harder one to build. Decades of documented harm have given
        many communities good reasons to be cautious around medical institutions. That caution
        doesn't dissolve because a nonprofit shows up with good intentions. It dissolves, slowly,
        through consistency — showing up in the same neighborhoods more than once, partnering with
        the community organizations already trusted there, and telling the truth even when the
        truth is complicated.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        None of this replaces the need for better access to nephrologists, fairer transplant
        waitlists, or the research that will one day make diseases like FSGS more treatable.
        Transplantation equity remains part of where this organization is headed. But prevention
        is only possible before a crisis, not during one — which is why education has to come
        first, not instead.
      </p>

      <!-- LOOKING FORWARD -->
      <p class="article-kicker chapter reveal">Looking Forward</p>
      <h2 class="article-heading reveal">A future geography can't predict.</h2>

      <p class="reveal">
        Picture a version of this country where a diagnosis of kidney disease doesn't depend on
        which zip code delivered it.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Where a teenager in the Bronx and a teenager in rural Georgia have the same chance of
        learning, early, what their family history means for their own kidneys. Where the first
        time someone hears the word <em>nephrologist</em> isn't in an emergency room. Where every
        patient's story — not just Robert's — becomes part of how the next person understands
        their own diagnosis, and maybe survives it a little more easily because of what came
        before them.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        That future doesn't require a miracle. It requires the same thing that changed Robert's
        life: someone willing to explain, someone willing to listen, and the belief that where you
        live should never be the reason you didn't know in time.
      </p>

      <p class="pull reveal article-closer">It starts here. It doesn't end here.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="on-dark section-tight cta-band">
    <div class="container cta-inner reveal">
      <h2 class="h2">Help advance health equity in kidney care.</h2>
      <div class="cluster gap-3" style="margin-top:var(--space-5);">
        <a href="/partner-with-us/" class="btn btn-steel-outline">Partner With Us</a>
        <a href="/donate/" class="btn btn-rust">Donate</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .narrow {
    max-width: 700px;
  }

  /* ---------- Hero (photo only) ---------- */

  .about-hero {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 58vh;
    overflow: clip;
  }

  .about-hero-bg {
    position: absolute;
    inset: -16% 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 132%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 30%;
    will-change: transform;
  }

  /* Flat tint, not a fade — the photo reads clearly right to its bottom
     edge, and the cut into the article below is deliberate, not hazy. */
  .about-hero-tint {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: rgba(29, 42, 30, 0.22);
  }

  .about-hero-placeholder {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: var(--space-6);
  }

  .about-hero-placeholder-note {
    max-width: 46ch;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--ink-fg-muted);
  }

  .about-hero-placeholder-note code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11.5px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  /* ---------- The article ---------- */

  .about-article {
    padding-top: 0;
    padding-bottom: clamp(var(--space-8), 8vw, var(--space-9));
  }

  .about-article .narrow p {
    color: var(--fg-soft);
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  /* ABOUT sits immediately under the hero, no gap, no gradient trick —
     just a clean, deliberate line between the photo and the article. */
  .article-kicker {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 13px;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--rust);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
  }

  .article-kicker:not(.chapter) {
    padding-top: clamp(var(--space-6), 6vw, var(--space-8));
  }

  .article-kicker.chapter {
    margin-top: clamp(var(--space-8), 8vw, var(--space-9));
  }

  /* The article's real headline — large, mixed case, literary, distinct
     from the site's usual uppercase-tracked .h2 on purpose: this one page
     reads as a feature, not a section list. */
  .article-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(34px, 5.5vw, 64px);
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--fg);
    max-width: 16ch;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-6);
  }

  .article-heading {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(28px, 4.2vw, 44px);
    line-height: 1.18;
    color: var(--fg);
    max-width: 18ch;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-5);
  }

  .about-article .pull {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
  }

  .article-closer {
    font-size: clamp(22px, 2.6vw, 28px);
    border-left-color: var(--steel);
  }

  /* ---------- Article images ---------- */

  .article-image {
    margin: clamp(var(--space-7), 6vw, var(--space-8)) auto;
    max-width: var(--content-max);
    padding-inline: clamp(20px, 5vw, 56px);
  }

  .article-image-inset {
    max-width: 460px;
  }

  .article-image figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }

  /* ---------- Where the name comes from (video figure) ---------- */

  .story-video-figure {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
  }

  .story-video-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }

  .cta-inner {
    text-align: center;
    max-width: 640px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { gsap } from "gsap";
  import { ScrollTrigger } from "gsap/ScrollTrigger";

  gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);

  const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
  const section = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-parallax-section]");
  const bg = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-parallax-bg]");

  // Gentle big-story parallax on the hero photo only — the image is sized
  // larger than its box (see .about-hero-bg) so the drift never reveals
  // empty space at the edges.
  if (section && bg && !prefersReducedMotion) {
    gsap.to(bg, {
      yPercent: 12,
      ease: "none",
      scrollTrigger: {
        trigger: section,
        start: "top top",
        end: "bottom top",
        scrub: true,
      },
    });
  }
</script>
